{# The shared checkable option list: ONE panel markup with two consumers — the
   property pills inside a dialog, and the inline edit control on a detail screen.
   Keeping it one component is what makes the dialog pattern and the inline
   pattern one agreed pattern rather than two that happen to look alike.

   IT SELECTS WHEN IT IS GIVEN A NAME, and is presentational when it is not.
   Without `input_name` the rows are exactly what they always were: `role="option"`
   with `aria-selected`, a marker box, and nothing a pointer or a keyboard can
   actually pick — which is what every caller before the facets needed, because
   only `option_picker` knew whether a pick writes immediately (inline) or when
   the form does (field), so the hidden inputs lived there. WITH `input_name`
   every row carries a real `<input>` of that name inside a `<label>`, at the
   position the marker box already occupies, checked from the same `selected`
   data. That control is the whole difference between displaying a selection and
   making one, and it is what makes a facet operable WITH JAVASCRIPT DISABLED —
   the same standard the panel's native `<details>` is held to.

   IT SUBMITS WHEN IT IS GIVEN `submit_name`, which is the third mode and the one
   that makes `option_picker`'s `inline` contract honest rather than worked
   around. That contract says the inline variant emits NO hidden inputs, "because
   the pick is the write" — so for an inline picker a value-carrying input is the
   wrong shape entirely: there is no later submit to carry it to. With
   `submit_name` each option row IS a named submit trigger of the enclosing form,
   `<button type="submit" name="{{ submit_name }}" value="{{ option.value }}">`,
   so picking a value and submitting it are one act. On the server the pair
   `name=value` is what identifies which row was pressed, which is the same
   mechanism a form's ordinary buttons use — several controls sharing one name,
   distinguished by value — so nothing new has to be invented to read it back.
   It also keeps the standard the rest of this panel is held to: a real submit
   button works with JavaScript disabled.

   THE THREE MODES ARE EXCLUSIVE AND `submit_name` WINS, because a row cannot be
   both a value to carry and a submission of that value; a caller that sets both
   has stated two intentions and the write-now one is the narrower.

   `formnovalidate` IS ON EVERY SUBMIT ROW, and it is a property of "the pick is
   the write" rather than a host's quirk. This control submits ONE field's value,
   and it sits in a form that may hold another field with a constraint of its own
   — a required person picker, say. The browser refuses to submit a form whose
   required field is empty, and it refuses BEFORE any script sees the event, so
   without this attribute an unrelated empty field silently disables every row
   here. The constraint itself is not weakened; only the pre-emption is dropped,
   and the field that owns the constraint still enforces it on its own submit.

   THE CLEAR ROW AND THE CREATE ROW ARE UNCHANGED BY THIS MODE, and both for
   reasons already written above: clearing is an ADDRESS and carries no control of
   any kind, and creating is an action that is already a real button. So the row
   order is the same in all three modes — clear first, options, create last.

   `multiple` marks the listbox multi-selectable. It paints nothing extra: in
   every mode an unselected row shows no marker at all and a selected row shows
   only the checkmark, while the marker keeps its fixed 16px space so labels
   never shift when the selection changes. `input_type` follows
   the same fact from the other side: `checkbox` for a field that holds several
   values, `radio` for one — so `multiple` is the ARIA half and `input_type` the
   form half of one statement about the FIELD.

   CLEARING IS A ROW OF THIS LIST, not a button above it and not a native
   `<option>` (action-pattern.md, rule 5). It is the first row, it carries the
   same marker machinery as every other row, and it shows the checkmark when
   nothing else is selected — so "no conductor" reads as a value the operator has
   chosen rather than as an absence they have to produce. That is why `clearable`
   lives here rather than on the picker: the row belongs to the listbox, so a
   clear control the picker owned would sit outside the listbox where no
   `role="option"` can reach it.

   IT IS THE ONE ROW THAT IS NOT A FORM CONTROL, and the previous line of this
   comment said the opposite — "it gets a control like every other row, carrying
   the EMPTY value: clearing is a value the operator picks." That was wrong, and
   wrong in a way that broke the screen it was written for. An empty value in a
   GET query is not the absence of a constraint: a search backend reads it as a
   constraint that nothing satisfies. Measured on the facet panel this list
   renders, `?state[]=coding` returned 5 rows and `?state[]=coding&group=` — the
   same query plus one clear row's own submission — returned 0. Because
   `clear_selected` is derived, EVERY facet with nothing chosen submitted one, on
   every submit.

   SO CLEARING IS AN ADDRESS, NOT A VALUE. Given `clear_url` the row is a LINK to
   the list without this field's constraint, which is the same affordance the
   `active-filters` chip's × already is and, deliberately, the same address —
   computed once by the caller so a chip and a row cannot disagree about what
   removal means. A link also keeps the standard the rest of this panel is held
   to: it works with JavaScript disabled, where a control unchecked by script
   would not. Without `clear_url` the row is the presentational marker it always
   was, which is the right reading when nothing is chosen and clearing is a
   no-op. The multi-value case loses nothing either way: every other row IS a
   real checkbox, so unchecking is native.

   `clear_selected` is DERIVED — nothing is selected iff no row says it is — so a
   scene cannot state a selection and a contradicting clear marker at the same
   time. It is computed with `column` + `in` rather than a loop, because a
   `{% set %}` inside a `{% for %}` does not survive the loop's scope. The three
   control settings below are computed for the same reason: they are facts about
   the LIST, not about a row, so they are established once, above the loop. #}
{% set clear = clear_label|default('— none —') %}
{% set clear_selected = true not in options|default([])|column('selected') %}
{% set trigger = submit_name is not empty %}
{% set control = not trigger and input_name is not empty %}
{% set control_type = input_type|default(multiple ? 'checkbox' : 'radio') %}
{% set control_name = input_name ~ (control_type == 'checkbox' ? '[]' : '') %}
{#- With `clear_control` the clear row of a single-value list is a radio
    posting "", so an entity form can empty the field. Opt-in, because for a
    facet an empty value matches nothing; a `clear_url` wins. #}
{% set clear_input = clear_control|default(false) and control and control_type == 'radio' and clear_url is empty %}
<ul{{ (attributes ?: create_attribute()).addClass([
    'option-list', 'm-0', 'p-0', 'list-none', 'flex', 'flex-col',
  ]) }}
  role="listbox"
  {%- if multiple %} aria-multiselectable="true"{% endif %}
  {%- if name %} data-option-list="{{ name }}"{% endif %}>
  {%- if clearable %}
  <li class="option-list__option option-list__option--clear flex items-center gap-[8px] px-[8px] py-[6px] rounded-xs cursor-pointer text-caption text-text-faint hover:bg-surface-3 hover:text-text"
    role="option"
    aria-selected="{{ clear_selected ? 'true' : 'false' }}"
    data-option-list-clear
    data-value="">
    {%- if clear_url is not empty %}<a class="option-list__clear flex items-center gap-[8px] w-full text-inherit no-underline" href="{{ clear_url }}">{% endif %}
    {%- if clear_input %}<label class="option-list__control flex items-center gap-2 w-full cursor-pointer">{% endif %}
    <span class="option-list__text flex flex-col min-w-0 flex-1">
      <span class="option-list__label truncate">{{ clear }}</span>
    </span>
    {%- if clear_input %}
    <input type="radio" name="{{ control_name }}" value=""
      class="option-list__input sr-only peer"{% if clear_selected %} checked{% endif %} />
    {%- endif %}
    {#- The clear row is not a form control, so it has no `peer`; its marker
        carries the checkmark only while nothing else is selected and is
        otherwise empty space. It paints no box in any mode, like every other
        row, and keeps its fixed 16px space so the label never shifts. #}
    {%- if clear_input %}
    <span class="option-list__marker inline-grid place-items-center w-4 h-4 shrink-0 mt-px text-primary [&>svg]:[visibility:hidden] peer-checked:[&>svg]:[visibility:visible]">
      {{- include('bb_theme:icon', { name: 'check', size: 'sm' }, with_context = false) -}}
    </span>
    </label>
    {%- else %}
    <span class="option-list__marker inline-grid place-items-center w-[16px] h-[16px] shrink-0 mt-[1px] text-primary">
      {%- if clear_selected %}{{ include('bb_theme:icon', { name: 'check', size: 'sm' }, with_context = false) }}{% endif %}
    </span>
    {%- endif %}
    {%- if clear_url is not empty %}</a>{% endif %}
  </li>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- for option in options|default([]) %}
  <li class="option-list__option flex items-center gap-[8px] px-[8px] py-[6px] rounded-xs cursor-pointer text-caption text-text hover:bg-surface-3 aria-selected:text-text"
    role="option"
    aria-selected="{{ option.selected ? 'true' : 'false' }}"
    data-value="{{ option.value }}">
    {%- if trigger %}<button type="submit" name="{{ submit_name }}" value="{{ option.value }}" formnovalidate
      class="option-list__submit flex items-center gap-[8px] w-full p-0 bg-transparent border-0 font-inherit text-caption text-inherit text-left cursor-pointer">{% endif %}
    {%- if control %}<label class="option-list__control flex items-center gap-[8px] w-full cursor-pointer">{% endif %}
    <span class="option-list__text flex flex-col min-w-0 flex-1">
      <span class="option-list__label truncate">{{ option.label }}</span>
      {%- if option.description %}<span class="option-list__description text-eyebrow text-text-faint">{{ option.description }}</span>{% endif %}
    </span>
    {%- if control %}
    <input type="{{ control_type }}" name="{{ control_name }}" value="{{ option.value }}"
      class="option-list__input sr-only peer"{% if option.selected %} checked{% endif %} />
    {%- endif %}
    {#- A real input already owns the checked state (see the docblock above); in
        control mode the marker is rendered FROM it with `peer-checked:` on the
        marker itself — a sibling of `.option-list__input`, which is what the
        `~` combinator `peer-checked:` compiles to needs — and never toggled by
        script, so glyph and control can never disagree.

        An unselected row shows no marker at all and a selected row shows only
        the checkmark. Only the child SVG is hidden, so the marker box itself
        keeps reserving its fixed space and labels never shift. ARBITRARY-PROPERTY
        `[&>svg]:[visibility:hidden]`, NOT THE `invisible` UTILITY, and that
        is a measured fix rather than a style choice: Drupal core's own
        `system.base.css` ships an UNLAYERED `.invisible{visibility:hidden}`,
        and unlayered CSS always wins over Tailwind's `@layer utilities`
        regardless of specificity — `invisible`/`peer-checked:visible`
        compiled correctly but never actually toggled anything on screen. The
        arbitrary form generates a class Tailwind owns outright, with no core
        name to collide with, and still reserves the box's own layout space
        either way, matching the presentational marker's static sizing. #}
    {%- set marker_mods = control ? ' [&>svg]:[visibility:hidden] peer-checked:[&>svg]:[visibility:visible]' : '' %}
    <span class="option-list__marker inline-grid place-items-center w-[16px] h-[16px] shrink-0 mt-[1px] text-primary{{ marker_mods }}">
      {%- if control or option.selected %}{{ include('bb_theme:icon', { name: 'check', size: 'sm' }, with_context = false) }}{% endif %}
    </span>
    {%- if control %}</label>{% endif %}
    {%- if trigger %}</button>{% endif %}
  </li>
  {%- endfor %}
  {#- CREATING is the mirror image of clearing, and the positions say which is which: the clear row is
      FIRST because it is a value the operator can pick, the create row is LAST because it is an
      action. That difference is also why this row is NOT a `role="option"` carrying `aria-selected`
      — a listbox option stands for a value, and announcing an action as a selectable value
      misdescribes it. It is a real <button> in a presentational row instead, which keeps the
      listbox's option set honest while leaving the row exactly where Linear puts it.

      Half of this is real and half is a boundary, deliberately. The affordance is rendered and
      reachable; it CREATES NOTHING YET, because nothing in this change posts. The write belongs to
      the ticket that gives these pickers an endpoint. The label below is the no-JavaScript form — a
      plain invitation — and the handler rewrites it to reflect the typed query. #}
  {%- if creatable %}
  <li class="option-list__create-row mt-[4px] pt-[4px] border-t border-border-2" role="presentation">
    <button type="button"
      class="option-list__create flex w-full items-center gap-[8px] px-[8px] py-[6px] rounded-xs bg-transparent border-0 text-caption text-primary text-left cursor-pointer hover:bg-surface-3"
      data-option-list-create
      data-create-noun="{{ create_label|default('item') }}">
      <span class="option-list__create-label truncate">+ New {{ create_label|default('item') }}</span>
    </button>
  </li>
  {%- endif %}
</ul>
